          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-ccb5785e-f46f-42d2-b1db-8cfc3d28491f\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/whatsnew/3.12.html\",\"title\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12 — Python 3.12.15 documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:08:20.357Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-33]\\nWhat’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\\n\\n[characters 48-202]\\nThis article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\\n\\n[characters 217-255]\\nPEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\\n\\n[characters 287-696]\\nPython 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\\n\\n[characters 1773-1804]\\nPython data model improvements:\\n\\n[characters 1806-1852]\\nPEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\\n\\n[characters 2474-2717]\\nReplace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\\n\\n[characters 2797-2833]\\nCPython implementation improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2868-2911]\\nCPython support for the Linux perf profiler\\n\\n[characters 2995-3040]\\nPEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\\n\\n[characters 3131-3250]\\nPEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\\n\\n[characters 3252-3475]\\nPEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\\n\\n[characters 5664-5957]\\nIn order to support these scoping semantics, a new kind of scope is introduced, the annotation scope. Annotation scopes behave for the most part like function scopes, but interact differently with enclosing class scopes. In Python 3.13, annotations will also be evaluated in annotation scopes.\\n\\n[characters 6138-6450]\\nPEP 701 lifts some restrictions on the usage of f-strings. Expression components inside f-strings can now be any valid Python expression, including strings reusing the same quote as the containing f-string, multi-line expressions, comments, backslashes, and unicode escape sequences. Let’s cover these in detail:\\n\\n[characters 6452-6735]\\nQuote reuse: in Python 3.11, reusing the same quotes as the enclosing f-string raises a SyntaxError, forcing the user to either use other available quotes (like using double quotes or triple quotes if the f-string uses single quotes). In Python 3.12, you can now do things like this:\\n\\n[characters 6921-7218]\\nNote that before this change there was no explicit limit in how f-strings can be nested, but the fact that string quotes cannot be reused inside the expression component of f-strings made it impossible to nest f-strings arbitrarily. In fact, this is the most nested f-string that could be written:\\n\\n[characters 7262-7398]\\nAs now f-strings can contain any valid Python expression inside expression components, it is now possible to nest f-strings arbitrarily:\\n\\n[characters 7444-7807]\\nMulti-line expressions and comments: In Python 3.11, f-string expressions must be defined in a single line, even if the expression within the f-string could normally span multiple lines (like literal lists being defined over multiple lines), making them harder to read. In Python 3.12 you can now define f-strings spanning multiple lines, and add inline comments:\\n\\n[characters 8119-8439]\\nBackslashes and unicode characters: before Python 3.12 f-string expressions couldn’t contain any \\\\ character. This also affected unicode escape sequences (such as \\\\N{snowman}) as these contain the \\\\N part that previously could not be part of expression components of f-strings. Now, you can define expressions like this:\\n\\n[characters 8732-9011]\\nAs a positive side-effect of how this feature has been implemented (by parsing f-strings with the PEG parser), now error messages for f-strings are more precise and include the exact location of the error. For example, in Python 3.11, the following f-string raises a SyntaxError:\\n\\n[characters 9168-9443]\\nbut the error message doesn’t include the exact location of the error within the line and also has the expression artificially surrounded by parentheses. In Python 3.12, as f-strings are parsed with the PEG parser, error messages can be more precise and show the entire line:\\n\\n[characters 9872-9990]\\nPEP 684 introduces a per-interpreter GIL, so that sub-interpreters may now be created with a unique GIL per interprete\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"October 2, 2023 Python 3.12 was released\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":33,\"text\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\"},{\"charStart\":48,\"charEnd\":202,\"text\":\"This article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\"},{\"charStart\":217,\"charEnd\":255,\"text\":\"PEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\"},{\"charStart\":287,\"charEnd\":696,\"text\":\"Python 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\"},{\"charStart\":1773,\"charEnd\":1804,\"text\":\"Python data model improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":1806,\"charEnd\":1852,\"text\":\"PEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\"},{\"charStart\":2474,\"charEnd\":2717,\"text\":\"Replace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\"},{\"charStart\":2797,\"charEnd\":2833,\"text\":\"CPython implementation improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2868,\"charEnd\":2911,\"text\":\"CPython support for the Linux perf profiler\"},{\"charStart\":2995,\"charEnd\":3040,\"text\":\"PEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\"},{\"charStart\":3131,\"charEnd\":3250,\"text\":\"PEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\"},{\"charStart\":3252,\"charEnd\":3475,\"text\":\"PEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\"},{\"charStart\":5664,\"charEnd\":5957,\"text\":\"In order to support these scoping semantics, a new kind of scope is introduced, the annotation scope. Annotation scopes behave for the most part like function scopes, but interact differently with enclosing class scopes. In Python 3.13, annotations will also be evaluated in annotation scopes.\"},{\"charStart\":6138,\"charEnd\":6450,\"text\":\"PEP 701 lifts some restrictions on the usage of f-strings. Expression components inside f-strings can now be any valid Python expression, including strings reusing the same quote as the containing f-string, multi-line expressions, comments, backslashes, and unicode escape sequences. Let’s cover these in detail:\"},{\"charStart\":6452,\"charEnd\":6735,\"text\":\"Quote reuse: in Python 3.11, reusing the same quotes as the enclosing f-string raises a SyntaxError, forcing the user to either use other available quotes (like using double quotes or triple quotes if the f-string uses single quotes). In Python 3.12, you can now do things like this:\"},{\"charStart\":6921,\"charEnd\":7218,\"text\":\"Note that before this change there was no explicit limit in how f-strings can be nested, but the fact that string quotes cannot be reused inside the expression component of f-strings made it impossible to nest f-strings arbitrarily. In fact, this is the most nested f-string that could be written:\"},{\"charStart\":7262,\"charEnd\":7398,\"text\":\"As now f-strings can contain any valid Python expression inside expression components, it is now possible to nest f-strings arbitrarily:\"},{\"charStart\":7444,\"charEnd\":7807,\"text\":\"Multi-line expressions and comments: In Python 3.11, f-string expressions must be defined in a single line, even if the expression within the f-string could normally span multiple lines (like literal lists being defined over multiple lines), making them harder to read. In Python 3.12 you can now define f-strings spanning multiple lines, and add inline comments:\"},{\"charStart\":8119,\"charEnd\":8439,\"text\":\"Backslashes and unicode characters: before Python 3.12 f-string expressions couldn’t contain any \\\\ character. This also affected unicode escape sequences (such as \\\\N{snowman}) as these contain the \\\\N part that previously could not be part of expression components of f-strings. Now, you can define expressions like this:\"},{\"charStart\":8732,\"charEnd\":9011,\"text\":\"As a positive side-effect of how this feature has been implemented (by parsing f-strings with the PEG parser), now error messages for f-strings are more precise and include the exact location of the error. For example, in Python 3.11, the following f-string raises a SyntaxError:\"},{\"charStart\":9168,\"charEnd\":9443,\"text\":\"but the error message doesn’t include the exact location of the error within the line and also has the expression artificially surrounded by parentheses. In Python 3.12, as f-strings are parsed with the PEG parser, error messages can be more precise and show the entire line:\"},{\"charStart\":9872,\"charEnd\":9990,\"text\":\"PEP 684 introduces a per-interpreter GIL, so that sub-interpreters may now be created with a unique GIL per interprete\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-32453ca3-ce97-4ece-8ea1-120b2180dc3f\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\be02ee38d3982f0d63a1fde99286ad6e6c6bb84f1674ceced8222269cc953289\\\\session-5469ded3-3257-4b59-af66-58ddf89ff324\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-ccb5785e-f46f-42d2-b1db-8cfc3d28491f.txt\"}"
