          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-ccb5785e-f46f-42d2-b1db-8cfc3d28491f\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/whatsnew/3.12.html\",\"title\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12 — Python 3.12.15 documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:08:20.357Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-33]\\nWhat’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\\n\\n[characters 48-202]\\nThis article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\\n\\n[characters 217-255]\\nPEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\\n\\n[characters 287-696]\\nPython 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\\n\\n[characters 698-975]\\nThe language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\\n\\n[characters 977-1444]\\nThis article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\\n\\n[characters 1608-1645]\\nPEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\\n\\n[characters 1679-1771]\\nImproved ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions for NameError, ImportError, and SyntaxError exceptions\\n\\n[characters 1773-1804]\\nPython data model improvements:\\n\\n[characters 1806-1852]\\nPEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\\n\\n[characters 1954-2017]\\nThe os module received several improvements for Windows support\\n\\n[characters 2082-2182]\\nisinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\\n\\n[characters 2184-2294]\\nThe asyncio package has had a number of performance improvements, with some benchmarks showing a 75% speed up.\\n\\n[characters 2356-2448]\\nDue to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\\n\\n[characters 2474-2717]\\nReplace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\\n\\n[characters 2719-2738]\\nC API improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2740-2768]\\nPEP 697, unstable C API tier\\n\\n[characters 2797-2833]\\nCPython implementation improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2868-2911]\\nCPython support for the Linux perf profiler\\n\\n[characters 2913-2971]\\nImplement stack overflow protection on supported platforms\\n\\n[characters 3131-3250]\\nPEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\\n\\n[characters 3252-3475]\\nPEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\\n\\n[characters 3477-3748]\\ngh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\\n\\n[characters 4228-4301]\\nclass list[T]:\\n    def __getitem__(self, index: int, /) -> T:\\n        ...\\n\\n[characters 4985-5222]\\nThe value of type aliases and the bound and constraints of type variables created through this syntax are evaluated only on demand (see lazy evaluation). This means type aliases are able to refer to other types defined later in the file.\\n\\n[characters 5224-5662]\\nType parameters declared through a type parameter list are visible within the scope of the declaration and any nested scopes, but not in the outer scope. For example, they can be used in the type annotations for the methods of a generic class or in the class body. However, they cannot be used in the module scope after the class is defined. See Type parameter lists for a detailed description of the runtime semantics of type parameters.\\n\\n[characters 5664-5957]\\nIn order to support these scoping semantics, a new kind of scope is introduced, the annotation scope. Annotation scopes behave for the most part like function scopes, but interact differently with enclosing class scopes. In Python 3.13, annotations will also be evaluated in annotation scopes.\\n\\n[characters 5959-5988]\\nSee PEP 695 for more details.\\n\\n[characters 6091-6128]\\nPEP 701: Syntactic formalization of f\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"per-interpreter GIL Python API is anticipated for 3.13 C-API Py_NewInterpreterFromConfig\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":33,\"text\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\"},{\"charStart\":48,\"charEnd\":202,\"text\":\"This article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\"},{\"charStart\":217,\"charEnd\":255,\"text\":\"PEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\"},{\"charStart\":287,\"charEnd\":696,\"text\":\"Python 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\"},{\"charStart\":698,\"charEnd\":975,\"text\":\"The language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\"},{\"charStart\":977,\"charEnd\":1444,\"text\":\"This article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\"},{\"charStart\":1608,\"charEnd\":1645,\"text\":\"PEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\"},{\"charStart\":1679,\"charEnd\":1771,\"text\":\"Improved ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions for NameError, ImportError, and SyntaxError exceptions\"},{\"charStart\":1773,\"charEnd\":1804,\"text\":\"Python data model improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":1806,\"charEnd\":1852,\"text\":\"PEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\"},{\"charStart\":1954,\"charEnd\":2017,\"text\":\"The os module received several improvements for Windows support\"},{\"charStart\":2082,\"charEnd\":2182,\"text\":\"isinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\"},{\"charStart\":2184,\"charEnd\":2294,\"text\":\"The asyncio package has had a number of performance improvements, with some benchmarks showing a 75% speed up.\"},{\"charStart\":2356,\"charEnd\":2448,\"text\":\"Due to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\"},{\"charStart\":2474,\"charEnd\":2717,\"text\":\"Replace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\"},{\"charStart\":2719,\"charEnd\":2738,\"text\":\"C API improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2740,\"charEnd\":2768,\"text\":\"PEP 697, unstable C API tier\"},{\"charStart\":2797,\"charEnd\":2833,\"text\":\"CPython implementation improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2868,\"charEnd\":2911,\"text\":\"CPython support for the Linux perf profiler\"},{\"charStart\":2913,\"charEnd\":2971,\"text\":\"Implement stack overflow protection on supported platforms\"},{\"charStart\":3131,\"charEnd\":3250,\"text\":\"PEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\"},{\"charStart\":3252,\"charEnd\":3475,\"text\":\"PEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\"},{\"charStart\":3477,\"charEnd\":3748,\"text\":\"gh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\"},{\"charStart\":4228,\"charEnd\":4301,\"text\":\"class list[T]:\\n    def __getitem__(self, index: int, /) -> T:\\n        ...\"},{\"charStart\":4985,\"charEnd\":5222,\"text\":\"The value of type aliases and the bound and constraints of type variables created through this syntax are evaluated only on demand (see lazy evaluation). This means type aliases are able to refer to other types defined later in the file.\"},{\"charStart\":5224,\"charEnd\":5662,\"text\":\"Type parameters declared through a type parameter list are visible within the scope of the declaration and any nested scopes, but not in the outer scope. For example, they can be used in the type annotations for the methods of a generic class or in the class body. However, they cannot be used in the module scope after the class is defined. See Type parameter lists for a detailed description of the runtime semantics of type parameters.\"},{\"charStart\":5664,\"charEnd\":5957,\"text\":\"In order to support these scoping semantics, a new kind of scope is introduced, the annotation scope. Annotation scopes behave for the most part like function scopes, but interact differently with enclosing class scopes. In Python 3.13, annotations will also be evaluated in annotation scopes.\"},{\"charStart\":5959,\"charEnd\":5988,\"text\":\"See PEP 695 for more details.\"},{\"charStart\":6091,\"charEnd\":6128,\"text\":\"PEP 701: Syntactic formalization of f\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-32453ca3-ce97-4ece-8ea1-120b2180dc3f\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\be02ee38d3982f0d63a1fde99286ad6e6c6bb84f1674ceced8222269cc953289\\\\session-5469ded3-3257-4b59-af66-58ddf89ff324\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-ccb5785e-f46f-42d2-b1db-8cfc3d28491f.txt\"}"
