          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-ccb5785e-f46f-42d2-b1db-8cfc3d28491f\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/whatsnew/3.12.html\",\"title\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12 — Python 3.12.15 documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:08:20.357Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-33]\\nWhat’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\\n\\n[characters 48-202]\\nThis article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\\n\\n[characters 217-255]\\nPEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\\n\\n[characters 257-285]\\nSummary – Release highlights\\n\\n[characters 287-696]\\nPython 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\\n\\n[characters 698-975]\\nThe language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\\n\\n[characters 977-1444]\\nThis article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\\n\\n[characters 1647-1677]\\nPEP 669, low impact monitoring\\n\\n[characters 1679-1771]\\nImproved ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions for NameError, ImportError, and SyntaxError exceptions\\n\\n[characters 1773-1804]\\nPython data model improvements:\\n\\n[characters 1806-1852]\\nPEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\\n\\n[characters 2082-2182]\\nisinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\\n\\n[characters 2474-2717]\\nReplace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\\n\\n[characters 2797-2833]\\nCPython implementation improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2835-2866]\\nPEP 709, comprehension inlining\\n\\n[characters 2868-2911]\\nCPython support for the Linux perf profiler\\n\\n[characters 2913-2971]\\nImplement stack overflow protection on supported platforms\\n\\n[characters 2995-3040]\\nPEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\\n\\n[characters 3080-3129]\\nImportant deprecations, removals or restrictions:\\n\\n[characters 3131-3250]\\nPEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\\n\\n[characters 3252-3475]\\nPEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\\n\\n[characters 3477-3748]\\ngh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\\n\\n[characters 3750-3861]\\nThe asynchat, asyncore, and imp modules have been removed, along with several unittest.TestCase method aliases.\\n\\n[characters 3908-4083]\\nGeneric classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declarations of variance.\\n\\n[characters 4085-4181]\\nPEP 695 introduces a new, more compact and explicit way to create generic classes and functions:\\n\\n[characters 4303-4356]\\n    def append(self, element: T) -> None:\\n        ...\\n\\n[characters 4359-4494]\\nIn addition, the PEP introduces a new way to declare type aliases using the type statement, which creates an instance of TypeAliasType:\\n\\n[characters 4596-4724]\\nThe new syntax allows declaring TypeVarTuple and ParamSpec parameters, as well as TypeVar parameters with bounds or constraints:\\n\\n[characters 4726-4982]\\ntype IntFunc[**P] = Callable[P, int]  # ParamSpec\\ntype LabeledTuple[*Ts] = tuple[str, *Ts]  # TypeVarTuple\\ntype HashableSequence[T: Hashable] = Sequence[T]  # TypeVar with bound\\ntype IntOrStrSequence[T: (int, str)] = Sequence[T]  # TypeVar with constraints\\n\\n[characters 4985-5222]\\nThe value of type aliases and the bound and constraints of type variables created through this syntax are evaluated only on demand (see lazy evaluation). This means type aliases are able to refer to other types defined later in the file.\\n\\n[characters 5224-5306]\\nType parameters declared through a type parameter list are visible within the scop\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"Python 3.12 was released on October 2 2023 release date\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":33,\"text\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\"},{\"charStart\":48,\"charEnd\":202,\"text\":\"This article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\"},{\"charStart\":217,\"charEnd\":255,\"text\":\"PEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\"},{\"charStart\":257,\"charEnd\":285,\"text\":\"Summary – Release highlights\"},{\"charStart\":287,\"charEnd\":696,\"text\":\"Python 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\"},{\"charStart\":698,\"charEnd\":975,\"text\":\"The language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\"},{\"charStart\":977,\"charEnd\":1444,\"text\":\"This article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\"},{\"charStart\":1647,\"charEnd\":1677,\"text\":\"PEP 669, low impact monitoring\"},{\"charStart\":1679,\"charEnd\":1771,\"text\":\"Improved ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions for NameError, ImportError, and SyntaxError exceptions\"},{\"charStart\":1773,\"charEnd\":1804,\"text\":\"Python data model improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":1806,\"charEnd\":1852,\"text\":\"PEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\"},{\"charStart\":2082,\"charEnd\":2182,\"text\":\"isinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\"},{\"charStart\":2474,\"charEnd\":2717,\"text\":\"Replace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\"},{\"charStart\":2797,\"charEnd\":2833,\"text\":\"CPython implementation improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2835,\"charEnd\":2866,\"text\":\"PEP 709, comprehension inlining\"},{\"charStart\":2868,\"charEnd\":2911,\"text\":\"CPython support for the Linux perf profiler\"},{\"charStart\":2913,\"charEnd\":2971,\"text\":\"Implement stack overflow protection on supported platforms\"},{\"charStart\":2995,\"charEnd\":3040,\"text\":\"PEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\"},{\"charStart\":3080,\"charEnd\":3129,\"text\":\"Important deprecations, removals or restrictions:\"},{\"charStart\":3131,\"charEnd\":3250,\"text\":\"PEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\"},{\"charStart\":3252,\"charEnd\":3475,\"text\":\"PEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\"},{\"charStart\":3477,\"charEnd\":3748,\"text\":\"gh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\"},{\"charStart\":3750,\"charEnd\":3861,\"text\":\"The asynchat, asyncore, and imp modules have been removed, along with several unittest.TestCase method aliases.\"},{\"charStart\":3908,\"charEnd\":4083,\"text\":\"Generic classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declarations of variance.\"},{\"charStart\":4085,\"charEnd\":4181,\"text\":\"PEP 695 introduces a new, more compact and explicit way to create generic classes and functions:\"},{\"charStart\":4303,\"charEnd\":4356,\"text\":\"    def append(self, element: T) -> None:\