          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-630c1226-628b-4cfb-9aa8-02074d2f0ff6\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/whatsnew/3.12.html\",\"title\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12 — Python 3.12.15 documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:09:50.935Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-33]\\nWhat’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\\n\\n[characters 48-202]\\nThis article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\\n\\n[characters 217-255]\\nPEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\\n\\n[characters 287-696]\\nPython 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\\n\\n[characters 698-975]\\nThe language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\\n\\n[characters 2082-2182]\\nisinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\\n\\n[characters 2474-2717]\\nReplace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\\n\\n[characters 2995-3040]\\nPEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\\n\\n[characters 3131-3250]\\nPEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\\n\\n[characters 3252-3475]\\nPEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\\n\\n[characters 3477-3748]\\ngh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\\n\\n[characters 3750-3861]\\nThe asynchat, asyncore, and imp modules have been removed, along with several unittest.TestCase method aliases.\\n\\n[characters 5224-5662]\\nType parameters declared through a type parameter list are visible within the scope of the declaration and any nested scopes, but not in the outer scope. For example, they can be used in the type annotations for the methods of a generic class or in the class body. However, they cannot be used in the module scope after the class is defined. See Type parameter lists for a detailed description of the runtime semantics of type parameters.\\n\\n[characters 6452-6735]\\nQuote reuse: in Python 3.11, reusing the same quotes as the enclosing f-string raises a SyntaxError, forcing the user to either use other available quotes (like using double quotes or triple quotes if the f-string uses single quotes). In Python 3.12, you can now do things like this:\\n\\n[characters 7220-7259]\\n>>> f\\\"\\\"\\\"{f'''{f'{f\\\"{1+1}\\\"}'}'''}\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n'2'\\n\\n[characters 7400-7441]\\n>>> f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{1+1}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"\\n'2'\\n\\n[characters 7444-7807]\\nMulti-line expressions and comments: In Python 3.11, f-string expressions must be defined in a single line, even if the expression within the f-string could normally span multiple lines (like literal lists being defined over multiple lines), making them harder to read. In Python 3.12 you can now define f-strings spanning multiple lines, and add inline comments:\\n\\n[characters 8119-8439]\\nBackslashes and unicode characters: before Python 3.12 f-string expressions couldn’t contain any \\\\ character. This also affected unicode escape sequences (such as \\\\N{snowman}) as these contain the \\\\N part that previously could not be part of expression components of f-strings. Now, you can define expressions like this:\\n\\n[characters 9168-9443]\\nbut the error message doesn’t include the exact location of the error within the line and also has the expression artificially surrounded by parentheses. In Python 3.12, as f-strings are parsed with the PEG parser, error messages can be more precise and show the entire line:\\n\\n[characters 9632-9838]\\n(Contributed by Pablo Galindo, Batuhan Taskaya, Lysandros Nikolaou, Cristián Maureira-Fredes and Marta Gómez in gh-102856. PEP written by Pablo Galindo, Batuhan Taskaya, Lysandros Nikolaou and Marta Gómez).\\n\\n[characters 10717-10762]\\n(Contributed by Eric Snow in gh-104210, etc.)\\n\\n[characters 11153-11196]\\n(Contributed by Mark Shannon in gh-103082.)\\n\\n[characters 11406-11426]\\nThe new collections.\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"October 2 2023 released changelog Removed ensurepip bundled setuptools wheel runtime dependency\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":33,\"text\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\"},{\"charStart\":48,\"charEnd\":202,\"text\":\"This article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\"},{\"charStart\":217,\"charEnd\":255,\"text\":\"PEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\"},{\"charStart\":287,\"charEnd\":696,\"text\":\"Python 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\"},{\"charStart\":698,\"charEnd\":975,\"text\":\"The language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\"},{\"charStart\":2082,\"charEnd\":2182,\"text\":\"isinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\"},{\"charStart\":2474,\"charEnd\":2717,\"text\":\"Replace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\"},{\"charStart\":2995,\"charEnd\":3040,\"text\":\"PEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\"},{\"charStart\":3131,\"charEnd\":3250,\"text\":\"PEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\"},{\"charStart\":3252,\"charEnd\":3475,\"text\":\"PEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\"},{\"charStart\":3477,\"charEnd\":3748,\"text\":\"gh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\"},{\"charStart\":3750,\"charEnd\":3861,\"text\":\"The asynchat, asyncore, and imp modules have been removed, along with several unittest.TestCase method aliases.\"},{\"charStart\":5224,\"charEnd\":5662,\"text\":\"Type parameters declared through a type parameter list are visible within the scope of the declaration and any nested scopes, but not in the outer scope. For example, they can be used in the type annotations for the methods of a generic class or in the class body. However, they cannot be used in the module scope after the class is defined. See Type parameter lists for a detailed description of the runtime semantics of type parameters.\"},{\"charStart\":6452,\"charEnd\":6735,\"text\":\"Quote reuse: in Python 3.11, reusing the same quotes as the enclosing f-string raises a SyntaxError, forcing the user to either use other available quotes (like using double quotes or triple quotes if the f-string uses single quotes). In Python 3.12, you can now do things like this:\"},{\"charStart\":7220,\"charEnd\":7259,\"text\":\">>> f\\\"\\\"\\\"{f'''{f'{f\\\"{1+1}\\\"}'}'''}\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n'2'\"},{\"charStart\":7400,\"charEnd\":7441,\"text\":\">>> f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{f\\\"{1+1}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"}\\\"\\n'2'\"},{\"charStart\":7444,\"charEnd\":7807,\"text\":\"Multi-line expressions and comments: In Python 3.11, f-string expressions must be defined in a single line, even if the expression within the f-string could normally span multiple lines (like literal lists being defined over multiple lines), making them harder to read. In Python 3.12 you can now define f-strings spanning multiple lines, and add inline comments:\"},{\"charStart\":8119,\"charEnd\":8439,\"text\":\"Backslashes and unicode characters: before Python 3.12 f-string expressions couldn’t contain any \\\\ character. This also affected unicode escape sequences (such as \\\\N{snowman}) as these contain the \\\\N part that previously could not be part of expression components of f-strings. Now, you can define expressions like this:\"},{\"charStart\":9168,\"charEnd\":9443,\"text\":\"but the error message doesn’t include the exact location of the error within the line and also has the expression artificially surrounded by parentheses. In Python 3.12, as f-strings are parsed with the PEG parser, error messages can be more precise and show the entire line:\"},{\"charStart\":9632,\"charEnd\":9838,\"text\":\"(Contributed by Pablo Galindo, Batuhan Taskaya, Lysandros Nikolaou, Cristián Maureira-Fredes and Marta Gómez in gh-102856. PEP written by Pablo Galindo, Batuhan Taskaya, Lysandros Nikolaou and Marta Gómez).\"},{\"charStart\":10717,\"charEnd\":10762,\"text\":\"(Contributed by Eric Snow in gh-104210, etc.)\"},{\"charStart\":11153,\"charEnd\":11196,\"text\":\"(Contributed by Mark Shannon in gh-103082.)\"},{\"charStart\":11406,\"charEnd\":11426,\"text\":\"The new collections.\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-32453ca3-ce97-4ece-8ea1-120b2180dc3f\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\be02ee38d3982f0d63a1fde99286ad6e6c6bb84f1674ceced8222269cc953289\\\\session-5469ded3-3257-4b59-af66-58ddf89ff324\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-630c1226-628b-4cfb-9aa8-02074d2f0ff6.txt\"}"
