      "type": "tool-result",
      "toolCallId": "call_d0799c9e1aac49f499d703a6",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-bc09795d-ddf0-4e94-b4a3-fe2b7560bf21\",\"url\":\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0684/\",\"title\":\"PEP 684 – A Per-Interpreter GIL | peps.python.org\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:10:19.183Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-348]\\nPEP 684 – A Per-Interpreter GIL\\nPEP 684 – A Per-Interpreter GIL\\nAuthor:\\nEric Snow <ericsnowcurrently at gmail.com>\\nDiscussions-To:\\nDiscourse thread\\nStatus:\\nFinal\\nType:\\nStandards Track\\nRequires:\\n683\\nCreated:\\n08-Mar-2022\\nPython-Version:\\n3.12\\nPost-History:\\n08-Mar-2022, 29-Sep-2022, 28-Oct-2022\\nResolution:\\nDiscourse message\\nTable of Contents\\nAbstract\\n\\n[characters 350-841]\\nSince Python 1.5 (1997), CPython users can run multiple interpreters in the same process. However, interpreters in the same process have always shared a significant amount of global state. This is a source of bugs, with a growing impact as more and more people use the feature. Furthermore, sufficient isolation would facilitate true multi-core parallelism, where interpreters no longer share the GIL. The changes outlined in this proposal will result in that level of interpreter isolation.\\n\\n[characters 863-932]\\nAt a high level, this proposal changes CPython in the following ways:\\n\\n[characters 934-1131]\\nstops sharing the GIL between interpreters, given sufficient isolation\\nadds several new interpreter config options for isolation settings\\nkeeps incompatible extensions from causing problems\\nThe GIL\\n\\n[characters 1133-1292]\\nThe GIL protects concurrent access to most of CPython’s runtime state. So all that GIL-protected global state must move to each interpreter before the GIL can.\\n\\n[characters 1425-1446]\\nCPython Runtime State\\n\\n[characters 1448-1711]\\nProperly isolating interpreters requires that most of CPython’s runtime state be stored in the PyInterpreterState struct. Currently, only a portion of it is; the rest is found either in C global variables or in _PyRuntimeState. Most of that will have to be moved.\\n\\n[characters 1713-2187]\\nThis directly coincides with an ongoing effort (of many years) to greatly reduce internal use of global variables and consolidate the runtime state into _PyRuntimeState and PyInterpreterState. (See Consolidating Runtime Global State below.) That project has significant merit on its own and has faced little controversy. So, while a per-interpreter GIL relies on the completion of that effort, that project should not be considered a part of this proposal–only a dependency.\\n\\n[characters 2221-2468]\\nCPython’s interpreters must be strictly isolated from each other, with few exceptions. To a large extent they already are. Each interpreter has its own copy of all modules, classes, functions, and variables. The CPython C-API docs explain further.\\n\\n[characters 2470-2610]\\nHowever, aside from what has already been mentioned (e.g. the GIL), there are a couple of ways in which interpreters still share some state.\\n\\n[characters 2612-2757]\\nFirst of all, some process-global resources (e.g. memory, file descriptors, environment variables) are shared. There are no plans to change this.\\n\\n[characters 2759-3049]\\nSecond, some isolation is faulty due to bugs or implementations that did not take multiple interpreters into account. This includes CPython’s runtime and the stdlib, as well as extension modules that rely on global variables. Bugs should be opened in these cases, as some already have been.\\n\\n[characters 3082-3469]\\nPEP 683 introduces immortal objects as a CPython-internal feature. With immortal objects, we can share any otherwise immutable global objects between all interpreters. Consequently, this PEP does not need to address how to deal with the various objects exposed in the public C-API. It also simplifies the question of what to do about the builtin static types. (See Global Objects below.)\\n\\n[characters 3471-3681]\\nBoth issues have alternate solutions, but everything is simpler with immortal objects. If PEP 683 is not accepted then this one will be updated with the alternatives. This lets us reduce noise in this proposal.\\n\\n[characters 3695-3994]\\nThe fundamental problem we’re solving here is a lack of true multi-core parallelism (for Python code) in the CPython runtime. The GIL is the cause. While it usually isn’t a problem in practice, at the very least it makes Python’s multi-core story murky, which makes the GIL a consistent distraction.\\n\\n[characters 4148-4254]\\nMost of the effort needed for a per-interpreter GIL has benefits that make those tasks worth doing anyway:\\n\\n[characters 4256-4506]\\nmakes multiple-interpreter behavior more reliable\\nhas led to fixes for long-standing runtime bugs that otherwise hadn’t been prioritized\\nhas been exposing (and inspiring fixes for) previously unknown runtime bugs\\nhas driven cleaner runtime initializa\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"per-interpreter GIL sub-interpreters unique GIL C-API Python API anticipated process-global resources shared no plans\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":348,\"text\":\"PEP 684 – A Per-Interpreter GIL\\nPEP 684 – A Per-Interpreter GIL\\nAuthor:\\nEric Snow <ericsnowcurrently at gmail.com>\\nDiscussions-To:\\nDiscourse thread\\nStatus:\\nFinal\\nType:\\nStandards Track\\nRequires:\\n683\\nCreated:\\n08-Mar-2022\\nPython-Version:\\n3.12\\nPost-History:\\n08-Mar-2022, 29-Sep-2022, 28-Oct-2022\\nResolution:\\nDiscourse message\\nTable of Contents\\nAbstract\"},{\"charStart\":350,\"charEnd\":841,\"text\":\"Since Python 1.5 (1997), CPython users can run multiple interpreters in the same process. However, interpreters in the same process have always shared a significant amount of global state. This is a source of bugs, with a growing impact as more and more people use the feature. Furthermore, sufficient isolation would facilitate true multi-core parallelism, where interpreters no longer share the GIL. The changes outlined in this proposal will result in that level of interpreter isolation.\"},{\"charStart\":863,\"charEnd\":932,\"text\":\"At a high level, this proposal changes CPython in the following ways:\"},{\"charStart\":934,\"charEnd\":1131,\"text\":\"stops sharing the GIL between interpreters, given sufficient isolation\\nadds several new interpreter config options for isolation settings\\nkeeps incompatible extensions from causing problems\\nThe GIL\"},{\"charStart\":1133,\"charEnd\":1292,\"text\":\"The GIL protects concurrent access to most of CPython’s runtime state. So all that GIL-protected global state must move to each interpreter before the GIL can.\"},{\"charStart\":1425,\"charEnd\":1446,\"text\":\"CPython Runtime State\"},{\"charStart\":1448,\"charEnd\":1711,\"text\":\"Properly isolating interpreters requires that most of CPython’s runtime state be stored in the PyInterpreterState struct. Currently, only a portion of it is; the rest is found either in C global variables or in _PyRuntimeState. Most of that will have to be moved.\"},{\"charStart\":1713,\"charEnd\":2187,\"text\":\"This directly coincides with an ongoing effort (of many years) to greatly reduce internal use of global variables and consolidate the runtime state into _PyRuntimeState and PyInterpreterState. (See Consolidating Runtime Global State below.) That project has significant merit on its own and has faced little controversy. So, while a per-interpreter GIL relies on the completion of that effort, that project should not be considered a part of this proposal–only a dependency.\"},{\"charStart\":2221,\"charEnd\":2468,\"text\":\"CPython’s interpreters must be strictly isolated from each other, with few exceptions. To a large extent they already are. Each interpreter has its own copy of all modules, classes, functions, and variables. The CPython C-API docs explain further.\"},{\"charStart\":2470,\"charEnd\":2610,\"text\":\"However, aside from what has already been mentioned (e.g. the GIL), there are a couple of ways in which interpreters still share some state.\"},{\"charStart\":2612,\"charEnd\":2757,\"text\":\"First of all, some process-global resources (e.g. memory, file descriptors, environment variables) are shared. There are no plans to change this.\"},{\"charStart\":2759,\"charEnd\":3049,\"text\":\"Second, some isolation is faulty due to bugs or implementations that did not take multiple interpreters into account. This includes CPython’s runtime and the stdlib, as well as extension modules that rely on global variables. Bugs should be opened in these cases, as some already have been.\"},{\"charStart\":3082,\"charEnd\":3469,\"text\":\"PEP 683 introduces immortal objects as a CPython-internal feature. With immortal objects, we can share any otherwise immutable global objects between all interpreters. Consequently, this PEP does not need to address how to deal with the various objects exposed in the public C-API. It also simplifies the question of what to do about the builtin static types. (See Global Objects below.)\"},{\"charStart\":3471,\"charEnd\":3681,\"text\":\"Both issues have alternate solutions, but everything is simpler with immortal objects. If PEP 683 is not accepted then this one will be updated with the alternatives. This lets us reduce noise in this proposal.\"},{\"charStart\":3695,\"charEnd\":3994,\"text\":\"The fundamental problem we’re solving here is a lack of true multi-core parallelism (for Python code) in the CPython runtime. The GIL is the cause. While it usually isn’t a problem in practice, at the very least it makes Python’s multi-core story murky, which makes the GIL a consistent distraction.\"},{\"charStart\":4148,\"charEnd\":4254,\"text\":\"Most of the effort needed for a per-interpreter GIL has benefits that make those tasks worth doing anyway:\"},{\"charStart\":4256,\"charEnd\":4506,\"text\":\"makes multiple-interpreter behavior more reliable\\nhas led to fixes for long-standing runtime bugs that otherwise hadn’t been prioritized\\nhas been exposing (and inspiring fixes for) previously unknown runtime bugs\\nhas driven cleaner runtime initializa\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-32453ca3-ce97-4ece-8ea1-120b2180dc3f\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\be02ee38d3982f0d63a1fde99286ad6e6c6bb84f1674ceced8222269cc953289\\\\session-5469ded3-3257-4b59-af66-58ddf89ff324\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-bc09795d-ddf0-4e94-b4a3-fe2b7560bf21.txt\"}"
