          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-630c1226-628b-4cfb-9aa8-02074d2f0ff6\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/whatsnew/3.12.html\",\"title\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12 — Python 3.12.15 documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:09:50.935Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-33]\\nWhat’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\\n\\n[characters 35-46]\\nAdam Turner\\n\\n[characters 48-202]\\nThis article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\\n\\n[characters 204-212]\\nSee also\\n\\n[characters 217-255]\\nPEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\\n\\n[characters 257-285]\\nSummary – Release highlights\\n\\n[characters 287-696]\\nPython 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\\n\\n[characters 698-975]\\nThe language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\\n\\n[characters 977-1444]\\nThis article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\\n\\n[characters 1446-1466]\\nNew syntax features:\\n\\n[characters 1468-1521]\\nPEP 695, type parameter syntax and the type statement\\n\\n[characters 1523-1544]\\nNew grammar features:\\n\\n[characters 1546-1579]\\nPEP 701, f-strings in the grammar\\n\\n[characters 1608-1645]\\nPEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\\n\\n[characters 1647-1677]\\nPEP 669, low impact monitoring\\n\\n[characters 1679-1771]\\nImproved ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions for NameError, ImportError, and SyntaxError exceptions\\n\\n[characters 1773-1804]\\nPython data model improvements:\\n\\n[characters 1806-1852]\\nPEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\\n\\n[characters 1854-1903]\\nSignificant improvements in the standard library:\\n\\n[characters 1905-1952]\\nThe pathlib.Path class now supports subclassing\\n\\n[characters 1954-2017]\\nThe os module received several improvements for Windows support\\n\\n[characters 2019-2080]\\nA command-line interface has been added to the sqlite3 module\\n\\n[characters 2082-2182]\\nisinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\\n\\n[characters 2184-2294]\\nThe asyncio package has had a number of performance improvements, with some benchmarks showing a 75% speed up.\\n\\n[characters 2296-2354]\\nA command-line interface has been added to the uuid module\\n\\n[characters 2356-2448]\\nDue to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\\n\\n[characters 2474-2717]\\nReplace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\\n\\n[characters 2719-2738]\\nC API improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2740-2768]\\nPEP 697, unstable C API tier\\n\\n[characters 2770-2795]\\nPEP 683, immortal objects\\n\\n[characters 2797-2833]\\nCPython implementation improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2868-2911]\\nCPython support for the Linux perf profiler\\n\\n[characters 2913-2971]\\nImplement stack overflow protection on supported platforms\\n\\n[characters 2973-2993]\\nNew typing features:\\n\\n[characters 2995-3040]\\nPEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\\n\\n[characters 3042-3078]\\nPEP 698, typing.override() decorator\\n\\n[characters 3080-3129]\\nImportant deprecations, removals or restrictions:\\n\\n[characters 3131-3250]\\nPEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\\n\\n[characters 3252-3475]\\nPEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\\n\\n[characters 3477-3748]\\ngh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\\n\\n[characters 3750-3861]\\nThe asynchat, asyncore, and imp modules have been removed, along with several unittest.TestCase method aliases.\\n\\n[characters 3863-3906]\\nNew Features\\nPEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax\\n\\n[characters 3908-4083]\\nGeneric classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declarations of variance.\\n\\n[characters 4085-4181]\\nPEP 695 introduces a new, more compact and explicit way to create generic classes and functions:\\n\\n[characters 4183-4226]\\ndef max[T](args: Iterable[T]) -> T:\\n    ...\\n\\n[characters 4228-4273]\\nclass list[T]:\\n    def __getitem__(self, inde\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"Remove the bundled setuptools wheel from ensurepip stop installing setuptools environments created by venv no longer provided by default relying at runtime declared as a dependency installed separately\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":33,\"text\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\"},{\"charStart\":35,\"charEnd\":46,\"text\":\"Adam Turner\"},{\"charStart\":48,\"charEnd\":202,\"text\":\"This article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\"},{\"charStart\":204,\"charEnd\":212,\"text\":\"See also\"},{\"charStart\":217,\"charEnd\":255,\"text\":\"PEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\"},{\"charStart\":257,\"charEnd\":285,\"text\":\"Summary – Release highlights\"},{\"charStart\":287,\"charEnd\":696,\"text\":\"Python 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\"},{\"charStart\":698,\"charEnd\":975,\"text\":\"The language changes focus on usability, as f-strings have had many limitations removed and ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions continue to improve. The new type parameter syntax and type statement improve ergonomics for using generic types and type aliases with static type checkers.\"},{\"charStart\":977,\"charEnd\":1444,\"text\":\"This article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\"},{\"charStart\":1446,\"charEnd\":1466,\"text\":\"New syntax features:\"},{\"charStart\":1468,\"charEnd\":1521,\"text\":\"PEP 695, type parameter syntax and the type statement\"},{\"charStart\":1523,\"charEnd\":1544,\"text\":\"New grammar features:\"},{\"charStart\":1546,\"charEnd\":1579,\"text\":\"PEP 701, f-strings in the grammar\"},{\"charStart\":1608,\"charEnd\":1645,\"text\":\"PEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\"},{\"charStart\":1647,\"charEnd\":1677,\"text\":\"PEP 669, low impact monitoring\"},{\"charStart\":1679,\"charEnd\":1771,\"text\":\"Improved ‘Did you mean …’ suggestions for NameError, ImportError, and SyntaxError exceptions\"},{\"charStart\":1773,\"charEnd\":1804,\"text\":\"Python data model improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":1806,\"charEnd\":1852,\"text\":\"PEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\"},{\"charStart\":1854,\"charEnd\":1903,\"text\":\"Significant improvements in the standard library:\"},{\"charStart\":1905,\"charEnd\":1952,\"text\":\"The pathlib.Path class now supports subclassing\"},{\"charStart\":1954,\"charEnd\":2017,\"text\":\"The os module received several improvements for Windows support\"},{\"charStart\":2019,\"charEnd\":2080,\"text\":\"A command-line interface has been added to the sqlite3 module\"},{\"charStart\":2082,\"charEnd\":2182,\"text\":\"isinstance() checks against runtime-checkable protocols enjoy a speed up of between two and 20 times\"},{\"charStart\":2184,\"charEnd\":2294,\"text\":\"The asyncio package has had a number of performance improvements, with some benchmarks showing a 75% speed up.\"},{\"charStart\":2296,\"charEnd\":2354,\"text\":\"A command-line interface has been added to the uuid module\"},{\"charStart\":2356,\"charEnd\":2448,\"text\":\"Due to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\"},{\"charStart\":2474,\"charEnd\":2717,\"text\":\"Replace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\"},{\"charStart\":2719,\"charEnd\":2738,\"text\":\"C API improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2740,\"charEnd\":2768,\"text\":\"PEP 697, unstable C API tier\"},{\"charStart\":2770,\"charEnd\":2795,\"text\":\"PEP 683, immortal objects\"},{\"charStart\":2797,\"charEnd\":2833,\"text\":\"CPython implementation improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2868,\"charEnd\":2911,\"text\":\"CPython support for the Linux perf profiler\"},{\"charStart\":2913,\"charEnd\":2971,\"text\":\"Implement stack overflow protection on supported platforms\"},{\"charStart\":2973,\"charEnd\":2993,\"text\":\"New typing features:\"},{\"charStart\":2995,\"charEnd\":3040,\"text\":\"PEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\"},{\"charStart\":3042,\"charEnd\":3078,\"text\":\"PEP 698, typing.override() decorator\"},{\"charStart\":3080,\"charEnd\":3129,\"text\":\"Important deprecations, removals or restrictions:\"},{\"charStart\":3131,\"charEnd\":3250,\"text\":\"PEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\"},{\"charStart\":3252,\"charEnd\":3475,\"text\":\"PEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\"},{\"charStart\":3477,\"charEnd\":3748,\"text\":\"gh-95299: Do not pre-install setuptools in virtual environments created with venv. This means that distutils, setuptools, pkg_resources, and easy_install will no longer available by default; to access these run pip install setuptools in the activated virtual environment.\"},{\"charStart\":3750,\"charEnd\":3861,\"text\":\"The asynchat, asyncore, and imp modules have been removed, along with several unittest.TestCase method aliases.\"},{\"charStart\":3863,\"charEnd\":3906,\"text\":\"New Features\\nPEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax\"},{\"charStart\":3908,\"charEnd\":4083,\"text\":\"Generic classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declarations of variance.\"},{\"charStart\":4085,\"charEnd\":4181,\"text\":\"PEP 695 introduces a new, more compact and explicit way to create generic classes and functions:\"},{\"charStart\":4183,\"charEnd\":4226,\"text\":\"def max[T](args: Iterable[T]) -> T:\\n    ...\"},{\"charStart\":4228,\"charEnd\":4273,\"text\":\"class list[T]:\\n    def __getitem__(self, inde\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-32453ca3-ce97-4ece-8ea1-120b2180dc3f\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\be02ee38d3982f0d63a1fde99286ad6e6c6bb84f1674ceced8222269cc953289\\\\session-5469ded3-3257-4b59-af66-58ddf89ff324\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-630c1226-628b-4cfb-9aa8-02074d2f0ff6.txt\"}"
