          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-630c1226-628b-4cfb-9aa8-02074d2f0ff6\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/whatsnew/3.12.html\",\"title\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12 — Python 3.12.15 documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:09:50.935Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-33]\\nWhat’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\\n\\n[characters 48-202]\\nThis article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\\n\\n[characters 217-255]\\nPEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\\n\\n[characters 287-696]\\nPython 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\\n\\n[characters 977-1444]\\nThis article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\\n\\n[characters 1468-1521]\\nPEP 695, type parameter syntax and the type statement\\n\\n[characters 1546-1579]\\nPEP 701, f-strings in the grammar\\n\\n[characters 1581-1606]\\nInterpreter improvements:\\n\\n[characters 1608-1645]\\nPEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\\n\\n[characters 1647-1677]\\nPEP 669, low impact monitoring\\n\\n[characters 1773-1804]\\nPython data model improvements:\\n\\n[characters 1806-1852]\\nPEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\\n\\n[characters 2184-2294]\\nThe asyncio package has had a number of performance improvements, with some benchmarks showing a 75% speed up.\\n\\n[characters 2356-2448]\\nDue to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\\n\\n[characters 2474-2717]\\nReplace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\\n\\n[characters 2719-2738]\\nC API improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2740-2768]\\nPEP 697, unstable C API tier\\n\\n[characters 2770-2795]\\nPEP 683, immortal objects\\n\\n[characters 2797-2833]\\nCPython implementation improvements:\\n\\n[characters 2835-2866]\\nPEP 709, comprehension inlining\\n\\n[characters 2868-2911]\\nCPython support for the Linux perf profiler\\n\\n[characters 2995-3040]\\nPEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\\n\\n[characters 3042-3078]\\nPEP 698, typing.override() decorator\\n\\n[characters 3131-3250]\\nPEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\\n\\n[characters 3252-3475]\\nPEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\\n\\n[characters 3863-3906]\\nNew Features\\nPEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax\\n\\n[characters 3908-4083]\\nGeneric classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declarations of variance.\\n\\n[characters 4085-4181]\\nPEP 695 introduces a new, more compact and explicit way to create generic classes and functions:\\n\\n[characters 4359-4494]\\nIn addition, the PEP introduces a new way to declare type aliases using the type statement, which creates an instance of TypeAliasType:\\n\\n[characters 4985-5222]\\nThe value of type aliases and the bound and constraints of type variables created through this syntax are evaluated only on demand (see lazy evaluation). This means type aliases are able to refer to other types defined later in the file.\\n\\n[characters 5664-5957]\\nIn order to support these scoping semantics, a new kind of scope is introduced, the annotation scope. Annotation scopes behave for the most part like function scopes, but interact differently with enclosing class scopes. In Python 3.13, annotations will also be evaluated in annotation scopes.\\n\\n[characters 5959-5988]\\nSee PEP 695 for more details.\\n\\n[characters 5990-6089]\\n(PEP written by Eric Traut. Implementation by Jelle Zijlstra, Eric Traut, and others in gh-103764.)\\n\\n[characters 6091-6136]\\nPEP 701: Syntactic formalization of f-strings\\n\\n[characters 6138-6450]\\nPEP 701 lifts some restrictions on the usage of f-strings. Expression components inside f-strings can now be any valid Python expression, including strings reusing the same quote as the containing f-string, multi-line expressions, comments, backslashes, and unicode escape sequences. Let’s cover these in detail:\\n\\n[characters 6452-6678]\\nQuote reuse: in Python 3.11, reusing the same quotes as the enclosing f-string raises a SyntaxError, forcing the user to either use other available quotes (like using double quotes or triple quotes if the f-string uses single \",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"PEP 684 unique GIL per interpreter sub-interpreters own GIL Python API anticipated 3.13 C-API only\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":33,\"text\":\"What’s New In Python 3.12\\nEditor:\"},{\"charStart\":48,\"charEnd\":202,\"text\":\"This article explains the new features in Python 3.12, compared to 3.11. Python 3.12 was released on October 2, 2023. For full details, see the changelog.\"},{\"charStart\":217,\"charEnd\":255,\"text\":\"PEP 693 – Python 3.12 Release Schedule\"},{\"charStart\":287,\"charEnd\":696,\"text\":\"Python 3.12 is a stable release of the Python programming language, with a mix of changes to the language and the standard library. The library changes focus on cleaning up deprecated APIs, usability, and correctness. Of note, the distutils package has been removed from the standard library. Filesystem support in os and pathlib has seen a number of improvements, and several modules have better performance.\"},{\"charStart\":977,\"charEnd\":1444,\"text\":\"This article doesn’t attempt to provide a complete specification of all new features, but instead gives a convenient overview. For full details, you should refer to the documentation, such as the Library Reference and Language Reference. If you want to understand the complete implementation and design rationale for a change, refer to the PEP for a particular new feature; but note that PEPs usually are not kept up-to-date once a feature has been fully implemented.\"},{\"charStart\":1468,\"charEnd\":1521,\"text\":\"PEP 695, type parameter syntax and the type statement\"},{\"charStart\":1546,\"charEnd\":1579,\"text\":\"PEP 701, f-strings in the grammar\"},{\"charStart\":1581,\"charEnd\":1606,\"text\":\"Interpreter improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":1608,\"charEnd\":1645,\"text\":\"PEP 684, a unique per-interpreter GIL\"},{\"charStart\":1647,\"charEnd\":1677,\"text\":\"PEP 669, low impact monitoring\"},{\"charStart\":1773,\"charEnd\":1804,\"text\":\"Python data model improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":1806,\"charEnd\":1852,\"text\":\"PEP 688, using the buffer protocol from Python\"},{\"charStart\":2184,\"charEnd\":2294,\"text\":\"The asyncio package has had a number of performance improvements, with some benchmarks showing a 75% speed up.\"},{\"charStart\":2356,\"charEnd\":2448,\"text\":\"Due to the changes in PEP 701, producing tokens via the tokenize module is up to 64% faster.\"},{\"charStart\":2474,\"charEnd\":2717,\"text\":\"Replace the builtin hashlib implementations of SHA1, SHA3, SHA2-384, SHA2-512, and MD5 with formally verified code from the HACL* project. These builtin implementations remain as fallbacks that are only used when OpenSSL does not provide them.\"},{\"charStart\":2719,\"charEnd\":2738,\"text\":\"C API improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2740,\"charEnd\":2768,\"text\":\"PEP 697, unstable C API tier\"},{\"charStart\":2770,\"charEnd\":2795,\"text\":\"PEP 683, immortal objects\"},{\"charStart\":2797,\"charEnd\":2833,\"text\":\"CPython implementation improvements:\"},{\"charStart\":2835,\"charEnd\":2866,\"text\":\"PEP 709, comprehension inlining\"},{\"charStart\":2868,\"charEnd\":2911,\"text\":\"CPython support for the Linux perf profiler\"},{\"charStart\":2995,\"charEnd\":3040,\"text\":\"PEP 692, using TypedDict to annotate **kwargs\"},{\"charStart\":3042,\"charEnd\":3078,\"text\":\"PEP 698, typing.override() decorator\"},{\"charStart\":3131,\"charEnd\":3250,\"text\":\"PEP 623: Remove wstr from Unicode objects in Python’s C API, reducing the size of every str object by at least 8 bytes.\"},{\"charStart\":3252,\"charEnd\":3475,\"text\":\"PEP 632: Remove the distutils package. See the migration guide for advice replacing the APIs it provided. The third-party Setuptools package continues to provide distutils, if you still require it in Python 3.12 and beyond.\"},{\"charStart\":3863,\"charEnd\":3906,\"text\":\"New Features\\nPEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax\"},{\"charStart\":3908,\"charEnd\":4083,\"text\":\"Generic classes and functions under PEP 484 were declared using a verbose syntax that left the scope of type parameters unclear and required explicit declarations of variance.\"},{\"charStart\":4085,\"charEnd\":4181,\"text\":\"PEP 695 introduces a new, more compact and explicit way to create generic classes and functions:\"},{\"charStart\":4359,\"charEnd\":4494,\"text\":\"In addition, the PEP introduces a new way to declare type aliases using the type statement, which creates an instance of TypeAliasType:\"},{\"charStart\":4985,\"charEnd\":5222,\"text\":\"The value of type aliases and the bound and constraints of type variables created through this syntax are evaluated only on demand (see lazy evaluation). This means type aliases are able to refer to other types defined later in the file.\"},{\"charStart\":5664,\"charEnd\":5957,\"text\":\"In order to support these scoping semantics, a new kind of scope is introduced, the annotation scope. Annotation scopes behave for the most part like function scopes, but interact differently with enclosing class scopes. In Python 3.13, annotations will also be evaluated in annotation scopes.\"},{\"charStart\":5959,\"charEnd\":5988,\"text\":\"See PEP 695 for more details.\"},{\"charStart\":5990,\"charEnd\":6089,\"text\":\"(PEP written by Eric Traut. Implementation by Jelle Zijlstra, Eric Traut, and others in gh-103764.)\"},{\"charStart\":6091,\"charEnd\":6136,\"text\":\"PEP 701: Syntactic formalization of f-strings\"},{\"charStart\":6138,\"charEnd\":6450,\"text\":\"PEP 701 lifts some restrictions on the usage of f-strings. Expression components inside f-strings can now be any valid Python expression, including strings reusing the same quote as the containing f-string, multi-line expressions, comments, backslashes, and unicode escape sequences. Let’s cover these in detail:\"},{\"charStart\":6452,\"charEnd\":6678,\"text\":\"Quote reuse: in Python 3.11, reusing the same quotes as the enclosing f-string raises a SyntaxError, forcing the user to either use other available quotes (like using double quotes or triple quotes if the f-string uses single \"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-32453ca3-ce97-4ece-8ea1-120b2180dc3f\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\be02ee38d3982f0d63a1fde99286ad6e6c6bb84f1674ceced8222269cc953289\\\\session-5469ded3-3257-4b59-af66-58ddf89ff324\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-630c1226-628b-4cfb-9aa8-02074d2f0ff6.txt\"}"
