          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-c75e7572-2375-4a13-8fc8-cb92d511133f\",\"url\":\"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-0.html\",\"title\":\"TypeScript: Documentation - TypeScript 5.0\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:17:15.161Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-866]\\nGet Started\\nHandbook\\nReference\\nModules Reference\\nTutorials\\nWhat's New\\nTypeScript 6.0\\nTypeScript 5.9\\nTypeScript 5.8\\nTypeScript 5.7\\nTypeScript 5.6\\nTypeScript 5.5\\nTypeScript 5.4\\nTypeScript 5.3\\nTypeScript 5.2\\nTypeScript 5.1\\nTypeScript 5.0\\nTypeScript 4.9\\nTypeScript 4.8\\nTypeScript 4.7\\nTypeScript 4.6\\nTypeScript 4.5\\nTypeScript 4.4\\nTypeScript 4.3\\nTypeScript 4.2\\nTypeScript 4.1\\nTypeScript 4.0\\nTypeScript 3.9\\nTypeScript 3.8\\nTypeScript 3.7\\nTypeScript 3.6\\nTypeScript 3.5\\nTypeScript 3.4\\nTypeScript 3.3\\nTypeScript 3.2\\nTypeScript 3.1\\nTypeScript 3.0\\nTypeScript 2.9\\nTypeScript 2.8\\nTypeScript 2.7\\nTypeScript 2.6\\nTypeScript 2.5\\nTypeScript 2.4\\nTypeScript 2.3\\nTypeScript 2.2\\nTypeScript 2.1\\nTypeScript 2.0\\nTypeScript 1.8\\nTypeScript 1.7\\nTypeScript 1.6\\nTypeScript 1.5\\nTypeScript 1.4\\nTypeScript 1.3\\nTypeScript 1.1\\nDeclaration Files\\nJavaScript\\nProject Configuration\\nTypeScript 5.0\\nDecorators\\n\\n[characters 868-985]\\nDecorators are an upcoming ECMAScript feature that allow us to customize classes and their members in a reusable way.\\n\\n[characters 1897-2005]\\nThis is where decorators come in. We can write a function called loggedMethod that looks like the following:\\n\\n[characters 3454-3842]\\nWe didn’t mention it yet, but loggedMethod was defined with a second parameter. It’s called a “context object”, and it has some useful information about how the decorated method was declared - like whether it was a #private member, or static, or what the name of the method was. Let’s rewrite loggedMethod to take advantage of that and print out the name of the method that was decorated.\\n\\n[characters 4274-4526]\\nWe’re now using the context parameter - and that it’s the first thing in loggedMethod that has a type stricter than any and any[]. TypeScript provides a type called ClassMethodDecoratorContext that models the context object that method decorators take.\\n\\n[characters 6437-6768]\\nNotice that we stacked two decorators - @bound and @loggedMethod. These decorations run in “reverse order”. That is, @loggedMethod decorates the original method greet, and @bound decorates the result of @loggedMethod. In this example, it doesn’t matter - but it could if your decorators have side-effects or expect a certain order.\\n\\n[characters 6770-6867]\\nAlso worth noting - if you’d prefer stylistically, you can put these decorators on the same line.\\n\\n[characters 8315-8528]\\nDecorators can be used on more than just methods! They can be used on properties/fields, getters, setters, and auto-accessors. Even classes themselves can be decorated for things like subclassing and registration.\\n\\n[characters 8530-8627]\\nTo learn more about decorators in-depth, you can read up on Axel Rauschmayer’s extensive summary.\\n\\n[characters 8629-8717]\\nFor more information about the changes involved, you can view the original pull request.\\n\\n[characters 8719-8766]\\nDifferences with Experimental Legacy Decorators\\n\\n[characters 8768-9205]\\nIf you’ve been using TypeScript for a while, you might be aware of the fact that it’s had support for “experimental” decorators for years. While these experimental decorators have been incredibly useful, they modeled a much older version of the decorators proposal, and always required an opt-in compiler flag called --experimentalDecorators. Any attempt to use decorators in TypeScript without this flag used to prompt an error message.\\n\\n[characters 9207-9659]\\n--experimentalDecorators will continue to exist for the foreseeable future; however, without the flag, decorators will now be valid syntax for all new code. Outside of --experimentalDecorators, they will be type-checked and emitted differently. The type-checking rules and emit are sufficiently different that while decorators can be written to support both the old and new decorators behavior, any existing decorator functions are not likely to do so.\\n\\n[characters 9661-9847]\\nThis new decorators proposal is not compatible with --emitDecoratorMetadata, and it does not allow decorating parameters. Future ECMAScript proposals may be able to help bridge that gap.\\n\\n[characters 9849-10111]\\nOn a final note: in addition to allowing decorators to be placed before the export keyword, the proposal for decorators now provides the option of placing decorators after export or export default. The only exception is that mixing the two styles is not allowed.\\n\\n[characters 10113-10268]\\n// ✅ allowed\\n@register export default class Foo {\\n    // ...\\n}\\n// ✅ also allowed\\nexport default @register class Bar {\\n    // ...\\n}\\n// ❌ error - before *and\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"parameter decorators constructors type-checking experimentalDecorators breaking changes\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":866,\"text\":\"Get Started\\nHandbook\\nReference\\nModules Reference\\nTutorials\\nWhat's New\\nTypeScript 6.0\\nTypeScript 5.9\\nTypeScript 5.8\\nTypeScript 5.7\\nTypeScript 5.6\\nTypeScript 5.5\\nTypeScript 5.4\\nTypeScript 5.3\\nTypeScript 5.2\\nTypeScript 5.1\\nTypeScript 5.0\\nTypeScript 4.9\\nTypeScript 4.8\\nTypeScript 4.7\\nTypeScript 4.6\\nTypeScript 4.5\\nTypeScript 4.4\\nTypeScript 4.3\\nTypeScript 4.2\\nTypeScript 4.1\\nTypeScript 4.0\\nTypeScript 3.9\\nTypeScript 3.8\\nTypeScript 3.7\\nTypeScript 3.6\\nTypeScript 3.5\\nTypeScript 3.4\\nTypeScript 3.3\\nTypeScript 3.2\\nTypeScript 3.1\\nTypeScript 3.0\\nTypeScript 2.9\\nTypeScript 2.8\\nTypeScript 2.7\\nTypeScript 2.6\\nTypeScript 2.5\\nTypeScript 2.4\\nTypeScript 2.3\\nTypeScript 2.2\\nTypeScript 2.1\\nTypeScript 2.0\\nTypeScript 1.8\\nTypeScript 1.7\\nTypeScript 1.6\\nTypeScript 1.5\\nTypeScript 1.4\\nTypeScript 1.3\\nTypeScript 1.1\\nDeclaration Files\\nJavaScript\\nProject Configuration\\nTypeScript 5.0\\nDecorators\"},{\"charStart\":868,\"charEnd\":985,\"text\":\"Decorators are an upcoming ECMAScript feature that allow us to customize classes and their members in a reusable way.\"},{\"charStart\":1897,\"charEnd\":2005,\"text\":\"This is where decorators come in. We can write a function called loggedMethod that looks like the following:\"},{\"charStart\":3454,\"charEnd\":3842,\"text\":\"We didn’t mention it yet, but loggedMethod was defined with a second parameter. It’s called a “context object”, and it has some useful information about how the decorated method was declared - like whether it was a #private member, or static, or what the name of the method was. Let’s rewrite loggedMethod to take advantage of that and print out the name of the method that was decorated.\"},{\"charStart\":4274,\"charEnd\":4526,\"text\":\"We’re now using the context parameter - and that it’s the first thing in loggedMethod that has a type stricter than any and any[]. TypeScript provides a type called ClassMethodDecoratorContext that models the context object that method decorators take.\"},{\"charStart\":6437,\"charEnd\":6768,\"text\":\"Notice that we stacked two decorators - @bound and @loggedMethod. These decorations run in “reverse order”. That is, @loggedMethod decorates the original method greet, and @bound decorates the result of @loggedMethod. In this example, it doesn’t matter - but it could if your decorators have side-effects or expect a certain order.\"},{\"charStart\":6770,\"charEnd\":6867,\"text\":\"Also worth noting - if you’d prefer stylistically, you can put these decorators on the same line.\"},{\"charStart\":8315,\"charEnd\":8528,\"text\":\"Decorators can be used on more than just methods! They can be used on properties/fields, getters, setters, and auto-accessors. Even classes themselves can be decorated for things like subclassing and registration.\"},{\"charStart\":8530,\"charEnd\":8627,\"text\":\"To learn more about decorators in-depth, you can read up on Axel Rauschmayer’s extensive summary.\"},{\"charStart\":8629,\"charEnd\":8717,\"text\":\"For more information about the changes involved, you can view the original pull request.\"},{\"charStart\":8719,\"charEnd\":8766,\"text\":\"Differences with Experimental Legacy Decorators\"},{\"charStart\":8768,\"charEnd\":9205,\"text\":\"If you’ve been using TypeScript for a while, you might be aware of the fact that it’s had support for “experimental” decorators for years. While these experimental decorators have been incredibly useful, they modeled a much older version of the decorators proposal, and always required an opt-in compiler flag called --experimentalDecorators. Any attempt to use decorators in TypeScript without this flag used to prompt an error message.\"},{\"charStart\":9207,\"charEnd\":9659,\"text\":\"--experimentalDecorators will continue to exist for the foreseeable future; however, without the flag, decorators will now be valid syntax for all new code. Outside of --experimentalDecorators, they will be type-checked and emitted differently. The type-checking rules and emit are sufficiently different that while decorators can be written to support both the old and new decorators behavior, any existing decorator functions are not likely to do so.\"},{\"charStart\":9661,\"charEnd\":9847,\"text\":\"This new decorators proposal is not compatible with --emitDecoratorMetadata, and it does not allow decorating parameters. Future ECMAScript proposals may be able to help bridge that gap.\"},{\"charStart\":9849,\"charEnd\":10111,\"text\":\"On a final note: in addition to allowing decorators to be placed before the export keyword, the proposal for decorators now provides the option of placing decorators after export or export default. The only exception is that mixing the two styles is not allowed.\"},{\"charStart\":10113,\"charE