        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-43a8bc5d-f4e2-4d02-bf19-fc8a1320c47a\",\"url\":\"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/decorators.html\",\"title\":\"TypeScript: Documentation - Decorators\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:17:17.426Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-352]\\nGet Started\\nHandbook\\nReference\\nUtility Types\\nCheat Sheets\\nDecorators\\nDeclaration Merging\\nEnums\\nIterators and Generators\\nJSX\\nMixins\\nNamespaces\\nNamespaces and Modules\\nSymbols\\nTriple-Slash Directives\\nType Compatibility\\nType Inference\\nVariable Declaration\\nModules Reference\\nTutorials\\nWhat's New\\nDeclaration Files\\nJavaScript\\nProject Configuration\\nDecorators\\n\\n[characters 354-529]\\nNOTE  This document refers to an experimental stage 2 decorators implementation. Stage 3 decorator support is available since Typescript 5.0. See: Decorators in Typescript 5.0\\n\\n[characters 545-844]\\nWith the introduction of Classes in TypeScript and ES6, there now exist certain scenarios that require additional features to support annotating or modifying classes and class members. Decorators provide a way to add both annotations and a meta-programming syntax for class declarations and members.\\n\\n[characters 846-914]\\nFurther Reading (stage 2): A Complete Guide to TypeScript Decorators\\n\\n[characters 916-1074]\\nTo enable experimental support for decorators, you must enable the experimentalDecorators compiler option either on the command line or in your tsconfig.json:\\n\\n[characters 1091-1132]\\ntsc --target ES5 --experimentalDecorators\\n\\n[characters 1150-1247]\\n{\\n  \\\"compilerOptions\\\": {\\n    \\\"target\\\": \\\"ES5\\\",\\n    \\\"experimentalDecorators\\\": true\\n  }\\n}\\nDecorators\\n\\n[characters 1249-1543]\\nA Decorator is a special kind of declaration that can be attached to a class declaration, method, accessor, property, or parameter. Decorators use the form @expression, where expression must evaluate to a function that will be called at runtime with information about the decorated declaration.\\n\\n[characters 1634-1717]\\nfunction sealed(target) {\\n  // do something with 'target' ...\\n}\\nDecorator Factories\\n\\n[characters 2001-2252]\\nfunction color(value: string) {\\n  // this is the decorator factory, it sets up\\n  // the returned decorator function\\n  return function (target) {\\n    // this is the decorator\\n    // do something with 'target' and 'value'...\\n  };\\n}\\nDecorator Composition\\n\\n[characters 2254-2336]\\nMultiple decorators can be applied to a declaration, for example on a single line:\\n\\n[characters 2384-2617]\\nWhen multiple decorators apply to a single declaration, their evaluation is similar to function composition in mathematics. In this model, when composing functions f and g, the resulting composite (f ∘ g)(x) is equivalent to f(g(x)).\\n\\n[characters 2619-2736]\\nAs such, the following steps are performed when evaluating multiple decorators on a single declaration in TypeScript:\\n\\n[characters 2969-3427]\\nfunction first() {\\n  console.log(\\\"first(): factory evaluated\\\");\\n  return function (target: any, propertyKey: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {\\n    console.log(\\\"first(): called\\\");\\n  };\\n}\\n \\nfunction second() {\\n  console.log(\\\"second(): factory evaluated\\\");\\n  return function (target: any, propertyKey: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {\\n    console.log(\\\"second(): called\\\");\\n  };\\n}\\n \\nclass ExampleClass {\\n  @first()\\n  @second()\\n  method() {}\\n}\\nTry\\n\\n[characters 3586-3696]\\nThere is a well defined order to how decorators applied to various declarations inside of a class are applied:\\n\\n[characters 3698-4036]\\nParameter Decorators, followed by Method, Accessor, or Property Decorators are applied for each instance member.\\nParameter Decorators, followed by Method, Accessor, or Property Decorators are applied for each static member.\\nParameter Decorators are applied for the constructor.\\nClass Decorators are applied for the class.\\nClass Decorators\\n\\n[characters 4616-4803]\\nNOTE  Should you choose to return a new constructor function, you must take care to maintain the original prototype. The logic that applies decorators at runtime will not do this for you.\\n\\n[characters 5731-6484]\\nfunction reportableClassDecorator<T extends { new (...args: any[]): {} }>(constructor: T) {\\n  return class extends constructor {\\n    reportingURL = \\\"http://www...\\\";\\n  };\\n}\\n \\n@reportableClassDecorator\\nclass BugReport {\\n  type = \\\"report\\\";\\n  title: string;\\n \\n  constructor(t: string) {\\n    this.title = t;\\n  }\\n}\\n \\nconst bug = new BugReport(\\\"Needs dark mode\\\");\\nconsole.log(bug.title); // Prints \\\"Needs dark mode\\\"\\nconsole.log(bug.type); // Prints \\\"report\\\"\\n \\n// Note that the decorator _does not_ change the TypeScript type\\n// and so the new property `reportingURL` is not known\\n// to the type system:\\nbug.reportingURL;\\nProperty 'reportingURL' does not exist on type 'BugReport'.\\nProperty 'reportingURL' does not exist on type 'BugReport'.\\nTry\\nMethod Decorato\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"Parameter Decorators target parameterIndex descriptor\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":352,\"text\":\"Get Started\\nHandbook\\nReference\\nUtility Types\\nCheat Sheets\\nDecorators\\nDeclaration Merging\\nEnums\\nIterators and Generators\\nJSX\\nMixins\\nNamespaces\\nNamespaces and Modules\\nSymbols\\nTriple-Slash Directives\\nType Compatibility\\nType Inference\\nVariable Declaration\\nModules Reference\\nTutorials\\nWhat's New\\nDeclaration Files\\nJavaScript\\nProject Configuration\\nDecorators\"},{\"charStart\":354,\"charEnd\":529,\"text\":\"NOTE  This document refers to an experimental stage 2 decorators implementation. Stage 3 decorator support is available since Typescript 5.0. See: Decorators in Typescript 5.0\"},{\"charStart\":545,\"charEnd\":844,\"text\":\"With the introduction of Classes in TypeScript and ES6, there now exist certain scenarios that require additional features to support annotating or modifying classes and class members. Decorators provide a way to add both annotations and a meta-programming syntax for class declarations and members.\"},{\"charStart\":846,\"charEnd\":914,\"text\":\"Further Reading (stage 2): A Complete Guide to TypeScript Decorators\"},{\"charStart\":916,\"charEnd\":1074,\"text\":\"To enable experimental support for decorators, you must enable the experimentalDecorators compiler option either on the command line or in your tsconfig.json:\"},{\"charStart\":1091,\"charEnd\":1132,\"text\":\"tsc --target ES5 --experimentalDecorators\"},{\"charStart\":1150,\"charEnd\":1247,\"text\":\"{\\n  \\\"compilerOptions\\\": {\\n    \\\"target\\\": \\\"ES5\\\",\\n    \\\"experimentalDecorators\\\": true\\n  }\\n}\\nDecorators\"},{\"charStart\":1249,\"charEnd\":1543,\"text\":\"A Decorator is a special kind of declaration that can be attached to a class declaration, method, accessor, property, or parameter. Decorators use the form @expression, where expression must evaluate to a function that will be called at runtime with information about the decorated declaration.\"},{\"charStart\":1634,\"charEnd\":1717,\"text\":\"function sealed(target) {\\n  // do something with 'target' ...\\n}\\nDecorator Factories\"},{\"charStart\":2001,\"charEnd\":2252,\"text\":\"function color(value: string) {\\n  // this is the decorator factory, it sets up\\n  // the returned decorator function\\n  return function (target) {\\n    // this is the decorator\\n    // do something with 'target' and 'value'...\\n  };\\n}\\nDecorator Composition\"},{\"charStart\":2254,\"charEnd\":2336,\"text\":\"Multiple decorators can be applied to a declaration, for example on a single line:\"},{\"charStart\":2384,\"charEnd\":2617,\"text\":\"When multiple decorators apply to a single declaration, their evaluation is similar to function composition in mathematics. In this model, when composing functions f and g, the resulting composite (f ∘ g)(x) is equivalent to f(g(x)).\"},{\"charStart\":2619,\"charEnd\":2736,\"text\":\"As such, the following steps are performed when evaluating multiple decorators on a single declaration in TypeScript:\"},{\"charStart\":2969,\"charEnd\":3427,\"text\":\"function first() {\\n  console.log(\\\"first(): factory evaluated\\\");\\n  return function (target: any, propertyKey: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {\\n    console.log(\\\"first(): called\\\");\\n  };\\n}\\n \\nfunction second() {\\n  console.log(\\\"second(): factory evaluated\\\");\\n  return function (target: any, propertyKey: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {\\n    console.log(\\\"second(): called\\\");\\n  };\\n}\\n \\nclass ExampleClass {\\n  @first()\\n  @second()\\n  method() {}\\n}\\nTry\"},{\"charStart\":3586,\"charEnd\":3696,\"text\":\"There is a well defined order to how decorators applied to various declarations inside of a class are applied:\"},{\"charStart\":3698,\"charEnd\":4036,\"text\":\"Parameter Decorators, followed by Method, Accessor, or Property Decorators are applied for each instance member.\\nParameter Decorators, followed by Method, Accessor, or Property Decorators are applied for each static member.\\nParameter Decorators are applied for the constructor.\\nClass Decorators are applied for the class.\\nClass Decorators\"},{\"charStart\":4616,\"charEnd\":4803,\"text\":\"NOTE  Should you choose to return a new constructor function, you must take care to maintain the original prototype. The logic that applies decorators at runtime will not do this for you.\"},{\"charStart\":5731,\"charEnd\":6484,\"text\":\"function reportableClassDecorator<T extends { new (...args: any[]): {} }>(constructor: T) {\\n  return class extends constructor {\\n    reportingURL = \\\"http://www...\\\";\\n  };\\n}\\n \\n@reportableClassDecorator\\nclass BugReport {\\n  type = \\\"report\\\";\\n  title: string;\\n \\n  constructor(t: string) {\\n    this.title = t;\\n  }\\n}\\n \\nconst bug = new BugReport(\\\"Needs dark mode\\\");\\nconsole.log(bug.title); // Prints \\\"Needs dark mode\\\"\\nconsole.log(bug.type); // Prints \\\"report\\\"\\n \\n// Note that the decorator _does not_ change the TypeScript type\\n// and so the new property `reportingURL` is not known\\n// to the type system:\\nbug.reportingURL;\\nProperty 'reportingURL' does not exist on type 'BugReport'.\\nProperty 'reportingURL' does not exist on type 'BugReport'.\\nTry\\nMethod Decorato\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-32453ca3-ce97-4ece-8ea1-120b2180dc3f\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\be02ee38d3982f0d63a1fde99286ad6e6c6bb84f1674ceced8222269cc953289\\\\session-0149d2c9-9df6-4860-8425-7047a0c41387\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-43a8bc5d-f4e2-4d02-bf19-fc8a1320c47a.txt\"}"
